o the  viewport you can drag them in over here  to the left let's try that and you can  see immediately 140 images were loaded  you might be in the 2ds view where you  get to see like a visualization of your  images but i prefer the 1ds i'm going to  hold this down let go on 1ds  and you can drop down this little bit  here you can get a bunch of different  information on your image you can also  set control points if you're trying to  do that later but we're getting ahead of  ourselves  the first thing we want to do is align  these images and i want to show you all  how easy it is to do so how fast and how  automatic this process is  we can do it a number of different ways  but by going to the workflow tab  if we navigate over to align images we  can click that or we can just go to the  alignment tab where we have all of our  alignment settings and i'm just going to  click align images  wait a couple seconds  and look at this  we have  all of our different cameras here  lined up  you can see all these little dots in the  sky are  different camera positions you can see i  just  you know kind of  went along the formation of the cliff  i'm holding right click here to orbit  around this model  left click and hold will pan  and move myself around the scene and you  can scroll wheel to  zoom in as well  so as you can see if we kind of back up  far enough  we got a nice  point cloud view  of our 3d scan and this looks  super cool i can't wait to mesh this but  before we mesh it let's go ahead and  just line it up a little bit to the grid  so if we go to the 3d tools tab and we  hit set ground plane we'll get this  little gizmo and we can actually  just orient this  so it's lined up with our grid  we can uncheck that option and if we hit  set reconstruction region it'll reorient  this box now this box is what will be  meshed so if we  scrunch this in  right just this little section will be  mesh we don't want to mesh the entire  thing we want to mesh maybe just a  section but for our case i'm gonna go  ahead and  do  most of this thing  and let's go ahead and bring it bring it  up here  let's bring this down just a touch  and you can also hit the numpad  buttons to orient between left right top  bottom views and if you hit zero it'll  go back to  your main view  if for whatever reason  you guys did your alignment and you look  here on the left if we drop this down we  can see camera poses we have 140  registered images that means it used all  140 images to build  this point cloud  now if you have component 0 component 1  and component 2  then you guys are going to have to use  control points to bridge those  components together  and i'm not going to go over control  points in this video but the last video  i did on the full 3d scanning process i  go over control points in detail so be  sure to check it out if you guys are  having issues trying to combine  different components and set control  points  well let's go ahead and mesh this point  cloud we can go to the mesh model tab  here up at the top and we can mesh in  high detail but it's usually not  necessary normal detail should be fine  so let's click that and see what we get  be warned this can take  a while  bam and we have  a nice 3d mesh right here this is  looking really great we're going to  clean this up next but let me just walk  you through kind of what we're looking  at so if you are seeing kind of a point  cloud version  of your mesh that's because there's one  of two reasons either one  your view if you go to the view tab  it's set to vertices and it's just going  to look like a point cloud version  if you have sweet grayed out that's  because your model is above 40 million  tries and 40 million tries is the cap  for  reality captures real time render here  in the viewport  if it's above 40 million tries you'll  need to go to the view tab and use the  clipping box  to  only view a portion  of  your mesh  but in our case since it's under 40  million tries we can  view it in its full glory  we can turn off the clipping box  and let's clean this thing up if we go  to the 3d tools tab  we have a few different options here to  select and deselect  different parts of our mesh so what i'm  going to do  is actually grab the lasso tool  and kind of just draw  the area that i want to keep  and i'll navigate just around this bit  here  we'll come up the side of the mountain  and we're basically just going to chop  off  all this stuff that we don't want to  view i'll grab this rock  and this whole  bit here is nice so that's that's not  too bad there now we can take this and  invert the selection by clicking the  invert button there and we can filter  out our selection by clicking filter  selection  and that's going to make a new model  here under component zero now you see  all these nasty blue triangles back here  we can get rid of those  if we go to the advanced section here we  can uncheck the lasso tool and we can  select marginal triangles which those  are  or another way we can do it is select  the largest connected component which is  our mountain  and once again we can just invert that  selection and filter it out wonderful  now we just have our mountain mesh next  what we'll do is texture this thing and  i want to  talk about  a couple different ways to texture this  specifically the best way to texture  this so i'll go to the mesh model tab  and instead of just hitting texture  let's go to the settings tab and bring  this up  and i'll go ahead and  open up my unwrap parameters  so  i want to set my texture resolution to  the highest possible resolution  so that will be 16k  perfect the large triangle removal  threshold by default is set to 10 but  i'm gonna have this set to 100  and we want our style  by default its maximal texture count but  i'm going to set that to fixed texel  size and i'll make sure texel size is  set to  optimal it's going to make as many 16k  textures as it needs to in order to  texture this entire mountain face and  with these settings we'll just hit  texture  bam  look at that  look at this here's me i'm chilling up  here hanging from the top of this  mountain  heck yeah and we got ourselves a  textured  mountain amazing this right here is  gonna be our high quality photo scan  this is what we can make leds from this  is what we can simplify because we don't  want to bring in a 24 million triangle  mountain into unreal engine or into  blender or in the c4d we're going to  want to simplify this we're going to  unwrap it and then we're going to  reproject this high-res texture onto  our simplified mesh  so let's get started by closing out our  texture settings here  and under the 3d tools tab i will  navigate to the simplify tool  and we can set our target triangle count  i'm actually going to go with  5 million and we want to disable our  color reprojection and our normal  reprojection because i want to show you  all a method that gives us full control  over our texture reprojection in just a  second so let's go ahead and simplify  this model simplification is complete we  have our model 3 at 24 million tries and  our new simplified model 4 at 5 million  tries  perfect  so let's go ahead and close this out if  we drop down model 4 we can see we don't  have any textures like we talked about  what we're seeing here are actually just  vertex colors for reference here  so  the first thing we need to do before  reprojecting our texture is unwrap this  thing so if we go to the mesh model tab  we can click on the unwrap settings  now if we drop down our unwrap  parameters we can take a look at our  settings  we want 16k textures  for sure we want that large triangle  removal threshold set to 100 as well  and we want a maximal texture count  of one  and we'll unwrap this  awesome now this thing looks like uh  a real pain to look at actually  careful watch your eyes  so it is unwrapped it's prepped for the  texture reprojection  so let's go ahead and close our unwrap  tool  and let's go back to our 3d tools and  we'll click on texture reprojection  we're just going to ensure first off  that our source model which should be  model number three that is our  hi-res 24 million tri model that will be  our source  for our result that's this model right  here model number four our five million  tri model so make sure those are set  correctly we want color reprojection  enabled of course we want that color  texture and we also want to enable  normal reprojection we want a normal map  as well because a lot of the details we  lost  by simplifying this 5 million polygon  model we're going to bring back with  that normal map from our high res  model number three and with that we can  reproject  amazing now we have our simplified five  million  polygon model  with two textures on it we have one 16k  normal and one 16k color and now this is  optimized simplified and ready for  export and the easiest way to do that is  to go up to the top left here  and click export and i want to export an  fbx you can search for it here that's  one of my recently used formats i'll  just click on that save it all right so  for our settings let's go over these  uh we don't want to save the mesh by  parts so setting that to no is great fbx  that's what we chose that's perfect  um let us see texture settings right so  we definitely want to export textures  that's for sure  and we want a texture size of 16k you  can choose ak you can choose 32k if you  want 16k is plenty for us right now 8k  i'm sure would be great as well  now we can unravel the color and normal  layers as well we want to make sure  we're both exporting the color and the  normal texture  that's exactly what we want and as a  little bonus here  let's drop down the export  transformation settings if we set the  transformation preset to maya arnold and  unreal it's actually going to  scale your scene  by 100 that way we don't have to  scale our model every single time we  bring it into unreal and with that  we can hit ok so let's see what our scan  looks like in unreal five i'm also going  to show you how to build a master  material where you can control tint  saturation brightness and normal  intensity so i'm just gonna drag  our fbx here into the content browser  and we'll be prompted with this little  fbx import option here now usually i  always want to build a nanite mesh so  i'll check the nanite mesh box here and  for materials i'm going to drag in our  materials manually so i just set this to  do not create an unchecked import  materials  and we don't have to worry about import  scale because we already set that up in  reality capture when we exported and  we'll go ahead and import all fun facts  so if your little thumbnail display  looks strange and doesn't truly display  what you're bringing in if you go to  settings and check thumbnail edit mode  you can actually just click and drag to  orbit your thumbnail around  and when you're done you can just hit  done editing and i'll drag in  our 16k  diffuse map and i actually rendered out  an 8k normal map because a 16k normal  map crashes my version of unreal  maybe it works for you but 8k it is for  now so let's drag this out  oh my goodness right click and i'm using  wasd  to move around this thing is ginormous  which is exactly what we want this looks  awesome  but it needs a material so let's go  ahead and right click make a material  we'll just call this mountain master  material  and we'll go ahead and double click  and get this thing set up first i'll  just drag our diffuse and normal  textures into our scene  and we'll pipe the rgb in the base color  and the rgb for the normal into normal  now that is the most basic way to set  this up but i want to show you guys how  to get more control over all of this so  let's start with the normal map and make  a normal intensity setup the first thing  i'll do is hold 3 and make a constant 3  vector i'll navigate to the left side  here and change the blue channel to one